WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.190
Hi it's me, Michelle from Lab Muffin
with more science meets beauty. I keep

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:09.240
coming across recipes for DIY sunscreen
so I thought I'd make a video about them.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.650
There are rumours that store-bought
sunscreens are toxic and may even be

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.150
more harmful than the sun itself. So many
people are turning to DIY sunscreens

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:22.980
using what's considered the safest
sunscreen ingredient, zinc oxide, commonly

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:28.050
called zinc. The ingredients of a
store-bought zinc oxide sunscreen look

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:33.870
something like this in Australia: Active
ingredients: zinc oxide 15%. So you'd

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:38.540
think that mixing 15% zinc oxide into a
base would give SPF 50, right? And

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:43.350
sunscreen can be quite expensive as well,
so it's fantastic for your budget! That's

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:47.460
what people are thinking when they whip
up a DIY sunscreen recipe that looks

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.230
something like this: Oils, zinc oxide, stir
a few times.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:57.570
Sometimes people even slap an SPF label
on it and sell it. Unfortunately this is

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:02.540
a terrible idea.
There's a good reason that sunscreen is

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:07.700
treated legally as a medicine. People put
on sunscreen that's labelled SPF 30, go

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.950
into the sun's cancer-causing UV rays,
and expect the sunscreen to protect them.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.610
Because cancer is a pretty big deal, most
countries have lots of regulations

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:20.810
around sunscreen. Think about the other
things that protect you and your loved

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:24.470
ones from death. Would you put on a
radiation suit you found on Pinterest

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:29.540
before going into a nuclear zone? Would
you trust a DIY seatbelt? A DIY guide to

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:35.240
surgery? A DIY rollercoaster you found on
a blog? (Please don't say yes.) In case

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:40.550
you think skin cancer is no big deal,
here are some statistics on it.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:45.140
2 in 3 Australians have skin cancers by
70. There's one melanoma death in the US

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:49.580
every hour. Your risk of melanoma doubles
if you've had more than 5 sunburns.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:54.440
And daily sunscreen reduces your risk of
squamous cell carcinoma by 40% and

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:58.000
melanoma. by 50%.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:02.440
So now that you know how important sun
protection is, how do you make sure your

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:08.380
sunscreen works? Here's where SPF comes
in. SPF stands for Sun Protection Factor.

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:12.550
It tells you how much protection from
sunburn you get with sunscreen compared

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:17.770
to nothing at all. For example with SPF
30 sunscreen your skin can handle 30

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:23.350
times as much UV before burning, if you
apply the right amount. UV changes with

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:27.820
the time of day and with clouds, so SPF
testing usees special UV lamps in a

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:33.070
lab. Testing sunscreen on microscope
slides is unreliable, so it's tested on

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:37.690
actual human volunteers. 2 mg
of sunscreen are applied per square

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:42.070
centimetre of skin for the test. There's
also a broad-spectrum requirement in

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:45.520
most places, which means that the
sunscreen has a decent level of

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.770
protection against UVA. Sunburn is
mostly caused by shorter wavelength UVB,

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:56.200
but newer research has found that longer
wavelength UVA is also damaging. Both

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:01.570
contribute to skin aging and cancer.
Because proper SPF testing is quite

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:05.410
complicated it costs thousands of
dollars. There are other regulations

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:09.820
around drugs and that costs even more.
That's part of the reason why sunscreen

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.570
is expensive.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:17.840
Another reason sunscreen is expensive is
that it's very difficult to make a

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.489
product that reliably protects you from
the Sun, Unfortunately it's more

00:03:22.489 --> 00:03:26.739
complicated than just using the right
amount of UV protective ingredients,

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:31.069
Cosmetic formulators consider sunscreen
one of the most difficult products to

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:36.530
formulate because changing almost any
tiny thing will change the final SPF. The

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:41.569
TGA in Australia states in their testing
guidelines the SPF and other physical

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:45.470
properties of the sunscreen are affected
not only by the active ingredients

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:51.110
but also by the base. Cosmetic chemists
used the amount of active ingredient as

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:54.890
the starting point for a sunscreen. There's still a lot of trial and error

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:59.239
before they can make a sunscreen that
works. And it's not just working out what

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:04.069
to put in the other 85%. There are a ton
of factors to consider when making an

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:09.049
effective sunscreen - there are a whole
text books written on it. Here are some

00:04:09.049 --> 00:04:13.430
of the other factors that will affect
SPF: distribution of the active in the

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:18.859
sunscreen; interactions between different
ingredients; the pH of the sunscreen; how

00:04:18.859 --> 00:04:23.150
the sunscreen's been mixed; how the
sunscreen applies on skin; how the

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:28.520
sunscreen dries on the skin; separation
and degradation on the skin; settling and

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:33.080
degradation in the bottle over time. The
amount of active ingredient is only one

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:39.680
tiny part of the puzzle. Let's look at
just one factor, pH. Zinc oxide dissolves

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:45.080
below pH 7,and when it's dissolved it
won't give any protection. Your skin and

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:49.220
sweat are both below pH 7 so the pH of
the sunscreen will drop as you're

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:54.139
wearing it. Proper sunscreens are
formulated to avoid this problem. You can

00:04:54.139 --> 00:04:58.970
see here the different SPFs you get with
similar amounts of zinc oxide. 15%

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:04.310
zinc gives one sunscreen SPF 50 plus. 19%
zinc gives another sunscreen SPF 15.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:09.410
The BASF calculator, a tool that
cosmetic chemists use as a starting

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:15.860
point for formulations tells us that 20%
zinc gives us SPF 8 or 13. So there isn't

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:21.580
a straightforward relationship between
the amount of active ingredient and SPF.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:26.160
Let's say you've managed to get exactly
the same ingredients as a commercial

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.569
zinc oxide sunscreen and you're ready to
mix them together. You still won't

00:05:30.569 --> 00:05:34.860
necessarily be able to make a sunscreen
that works because, just like cooking, the

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.530
sunscreen ingredients need to be mixed
in the right way.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:41.729
Zinc oxide is a particularly annoying
ingredient to use in sunscreens because

00:05:41.729 --> 00:05:46.110
it's notorious for clumping up. This is a
problem because bigger particles mean

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:51.030
poorer coverage. For example, if you have
a cherry, it'll cover the area the size of

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:55.650
the cherry. But if you smash it up into
juice you can use it to cover a much

00:05:55.650 --> 00:06:01.320
larger area even though you've used the
same amount of cherry. Again, 15% zinc

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:05.639
oxide doesn't always give the same
protection. Here's a table from a zinc

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:09.330
oxide supplier showing the SPF values
you get from two different sizes of

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:14.340
particles. Even with the same percent
zinc oxide the SPF halves when you

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:18.870
change the particle size. Most DIY
sunscreen recipes don't recommend the

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:23.159
right size of zinc. But even if you buy
the right size of zinc, it doesn't mean

00:06:23.159 --> 00:06:28.050
it'll work because when zinc oxide clumps up it turns into bigger particles. How

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:32.729
can you stop the zinc oxide from
clumping? In commercial sunscreens zinc

00:06:32.729 --> 00:06:37.169
is usually coated with ingredients that
make it clump less, but DIY sunscreens use

00:06:37.169 --> 00:06:43.020
uncoated zinc oxide because the coatings
aren't "natural". Even when coated, zinc

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:47.250
oxide requires industrial homogenizers,
which are super powered electric mixers,

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:52.289
to spread the particles out. Both of
these sunscreens look even when you apply

00:06:52.289 --> 00:06:56.370
them, but when you look at them under a
microscope you can see the tiny holes

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:00.900
where you'll get no protection. In fact
you can get worse than no protection

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:06.570
because oils can increase UV damage. Zinc
also clumps up and settles over time,

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:12.030
even in some commercial sunscreens that
have passed SPF testing. So even if you

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:16.110
somehow managed to get the DIY sunscreen to work one time, the sunscreen

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:20.310
probably won't be the same when you use
it the second time. It can also clump up

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:26.580
as you apply it. Again the clumping might
not be visible. So in summary, a

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:27.080
sunscreen's effectiveness doesn't depend
on the amount of a single ingredient it

00:07:30.659 --> 00:07:34.440
contains. It doesn't even depend on the
amount of all the ingredients that

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.270
it contains. The process of making the sunscreen

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:41.440
makes a big difference too, and shaking
it in a jar or using an electric mixer

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.450
won't cut it. I mean, this bowl contains
the same cake ingredients as this

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:52.639
delicious delicious batter. But it won't
turn out the same when you bake it.

00:07:52.639 --> 00:07:57.810
Official SPF testing uses human
volunteers, but that doesn't mean testing

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:02.550
it on yourself is a good idea. The test
needs to be done in a reliable way that

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.810
will reflect whether it will be
effective for most people in real life.

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:09.990
Just like how my grandma smoked like a
chimney, had one kidney, and lived till

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:14.220
ninety, a sunscreen that you wore while
not burning isn't necessarily an

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:17.880
effective sunscreen. Were there any clouds that day? What was

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:22.350
the UV like at that time of year?
What was the time of day? Do you have sun

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:27.090
resistant skin? Did you go into the shade?
How often were you facing so that your

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:31.400
skin was in the shade? How much did you sweat? Did the sunscreen brush against

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:36.930
clothing? There are too many variables so
if it worked, all you can say is that it

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:42.599
worked for you in that exact situation
once. In proper SPF testing they control

00:08:42.599 --> 00:08:47.810
for all these variables. Sunburn is
also only one of the effects of UV.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:53.850
Sunburn mostly happens due to UVB but
UVA causes cancer, pigmentation

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:59.280
and wrinkles. Even if you don't get burned
using a DIY sunscreen, you're potentially

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:03.300
exposing yourself to some serious UVA
damage that you won't know about until

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:08.339
many years later. Sunscreen may be
expensive but it's expensive because

00:09:08.339 --> 00:09:13.340
it's effective, and it's much cheaper
than skin cancer.

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:17.420
There aren't any studies showing that
sunscreen chemicals cause more harm

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:20.270
than good. They definitely protect against skin

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:25.100
cancer and aging, according to the most
recent studies. There's some speculation

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:29.090
that sunscreen ingredients can cause
endocrine disruption, but the in vitro

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:33.500
and animal studies that found that use
way way more than you'd ever be exposed to.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.790
There's also concern about
nanoparticles, but the current evidence

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:41.930
shows that they don't pass through the
skin's surface. Allergic reactions are

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:46.850
real risk though, so make sure you patch
test if your skin is sensitive. Aside

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:51.350
from that, the UV protective benefits of
using a proper sunscreen outweigh the

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:53.770
risks.

00:09:54.649 --> 00:10:00.089
Unfortunately DIY sunscreen isn't
better than nothing. First, people stay in

00:10:00.089 --> 00:10:04.140
the sun longer if they think they're
protected, so you'd get more sun damage

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:10.470
overall. Secondly, oils don't give good
sun protection. In fact, oils can

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:14.490
sometimes increase sun damage. If there
are microscopic holes in your sunscreen

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:18.500
you'll have no protection there,
possibly worse than no protection.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:21.810
Finally zinc oxide itself can be a
problem.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:26.970
A lot of DIY recipes tell you to use
uncoated zinc oxide because it's

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:31.470
supposedly "more natural". But uncoated
zinc oxide is photoreactive.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:36.330
This means that when it's exposed to UV,
it will react with other substances to

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:41.190
produce free radicals, one of the big
causes of skin damage. Coating the zinc

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:45.630
oxide stops this from happening. While we
don't know whether the amount of free

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:50.850
radicals has a big effect on the skin,
you're potentially causing extra damage. By

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:54.899
wearing DIY sunscreen you might
actually give yourself more sun damage

00:10:54.899 --> 00:10:59.190
than wearing nothing at all! Keeping in
mind the scary skin cancer stats I

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:04.290
showed you earlier, this is pretty darn
scary. DIY sunscreens are a bad idea

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:08.040
because it's incredibly unlikely that
you've managed to make something that

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:12.870
can reliably protect you from
cancer-causing UV rays - and DIY sunscreen

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:15.720
can potentially expose you to more
damage than nothing at all.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:20.850
Save DIY for things that don't run the
risk of giving you cancer. If you're

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:25.050
still worried about the dangers of
sunscreen, then don't use them and practice

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:30.120
some avoidance. Stay in the shade or wear
sun protective clothing. Or you can

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:35.070
choose to only buy sunscreens containing
ingredients you regard as safe. Don't

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:39.570
repost DIY recipes and encourage other
people to try them, even if they work for

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:44.970
you. You don't want to be responsible for
their skin cancer. If you see people

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:49.470
selling DIY sunscreen illegally, you can
report it via the links I've provided in

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:54.540
the caption. I hope this video made sense
to you. If you enjoyed it please like it

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:58.950
and share it! You might also like to
check out my blog, watch the other videos

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:05.170
on my channel, and subscribe :)

